{"traceId":"e6883a18a637cb0d390f8cf3becd3a43","spans":{"8b24e21977bd9884":{"spanId":"8b24e21977bd9884","traceId":"e6883a18a637cb0d390f8cf3becd3a43","parentSpanId":"2ce71232c617e45f","startTime":1753146065552,"endTime":1753146088529.4224,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"googleai/gemini-2.5-pro","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{googleai/gemini-2.5-pro,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code entirely within the Amazon Web Services (AWS) ecosystem. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure application that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of AWS, and specifically, the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI).\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYour access to the AWS CLI will be connected to the user's AWS account, allowing you to create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up AWS services, uploading files like code or configuration files, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n### Example Flow of Work\\n\\nYou carry the following example with you as a stellar example of firm operations but understand that under no circumstances is this a catch-all or a procedure to be followed to the letter. It is only a greatly generalized demonstration indicating how a firm MIGHT handle the following initial user instruction:\\n\\n#### Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\n\\\"I am very interested in building a website for my neighborhood HOA. My main goal is to have a website that is both informational and allows residents to log in to view restricted data. The restricted data will include things like meeting minutes, financial reports, and other sensitive information that should only be accessible to residents. Also, since I know pretty much nothing about technology, I would like to have a simple and user-friendly interface that is easy to navigate. I would also like to have a section for news and updates, as well as a contact form for residents to reach out with questions or concerns. Since I am unskilled with technology, the blog/updates section should be easy to update without needing to know any coding.\\\"\\n\\n#### Example Work Flow for Example Initial User Instruction\\n\\nThe Chief-rank agent is initialized with the user's instruction.\\n\\nThe Chief determines that the request is broad and begins the Planning Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief decides to recruit two high-level subordinates using the RECRUIT call:\\n\\nA Director of Backend Engineering, tasked with planning the entire cloud infrastructure, authentication, and data storage.\\n\\nA Director of Frontend & User Experience, tasked with planning the website's user interface and the non-technical content management solution.\\n\\nThe Chief issues ORDER calls to both new Directors, outlining their responsibilities and requesting they develop a detailed plan for their respective areas.\\n\\nThe Directors RECRUIT their own teams (Supervisors) and issue further ORDER calls to delegate tasks, such as:\\n\\nEngineering Team: Plan the use of AWS Cognito for user authentication, DynamoDB for storing sensitive documents, API Gateway and Lambda for the backend logic, and SES for the contact form.\\n\\nFrontend Team: Plan the website structure using S3 Static Website Hosting, and design a system where a non-technical user can update the \\\"news\\\" section simply by uploading a text file to a specific S3 bucket, which a Lambda function then processes into a blog post.\\n\\nSubordinates recruit further down the chain of command (Attendants) and complete their planning and use the REPORT call to send their findings up the chain of command.\\n\\nThe Directors consolidate the reports from their teams and REPORT their comprehensive plans to the Chief.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) synthesizes the plans from both Directors into a single, master plan: the Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP). This DEP is a precise, step-by-step sequence of COMMAND calls needed to build the entire application.\\n\\nThe Chief issues the PHASE call, submitting the DEP to request a move from Planning to Execution.\\n\\nThe Partagaz D oversight group reviews the DEP. Upon approval, the system transitions to the Execution Phase.\\n\\nThe Chief (or an assigned subordinate) begins executing the DEP by issuing a series of COMMAND calls. Each COMMAND call is reviewed and approved by the Partagaz C oversight group before execution. Example commands might include:\\n\\n```\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket my-hoa-website-bucket ...\\n\\naws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name \\\"HOA_Residents\\\" ...\\n\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name \\\"HOA_Documents\\\" ...\\n\\naws lambda create-function --function-name \\\"ContactFormHandler\\\" ...\\n\\naws s3 cp ./website/index.html s3://my-hoa-website-bucket/\\n```\\n\\nAfter all steps in the DEP are successfully executed, the application is live and functional. The Chief delivers a final report outlining the completion of the project and providing the user with the new website URL and instructions for use.\\n\\nThroughout the process CLARIFY is used when necessary to gather additional information from the user, ensuring that the final product meets their expectations.\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Director-rank agent, the second-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Director-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Supervisor-rank agents, who themselves are fairly high-level actors who manage other agents. You are responsible for overseeing major operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to the Chief-rank agent, who is the highest authority in the firm.\\n\\nAs a #2 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Supervisor-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Director, all of your recruitments are Supervisor-rank agents. These are fairly high-level agents who will recruit other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" and middle managers down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Diamonds \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Diamonds means that you are predisposed to be more of a managing type, a delegator, one who thinks openly and creatively about the big picture, and who is not afraid to take risks. You are generally predisposed to be a leader, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Diamonds has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###frontend-plan-01###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 2\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to P (2) with sequence ID frontend-plan-01:\\\\n\\\\n]Welcome, Director. Your primary responsibility is to develop a comprehensive plan for the frontend and user experience of the HOA website. The user has emphasized the need for a simple, user-friendly, and easy-to-navigate interface. Your plan should cover the public-facing informational pages, the secure resident-only area, and the user interaction with the contact form and news feed. Critically, you must design the user-facing process for the 'no-code' news update system. Your plan should specify the use of S3 for static website hosting, with CloudFront for content delivery and SSL. The final report should be a detailed plan outlining the site structure, user flows, and client-side interactions with the backend services. Recruit subordinates as necessary to complete this task and report back with your complete plan.\\\"\\n}\\n###frontend-plan-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. I have received my directive from the Chief. My responsibility is to create a comprehensive frontend plan for the HOA website, focusing on user experience, security, and ease of use, particularly for the non-technical user who will be managing the news updates.\\n\\nMy strategy will be to delegate the detailed planning to a team of specialized Supervisors. I will break down the project into three core areas and recruit a Supervisor for each. This will ensure that every aspect of the Chief's order is addressed with focused expertise.\\n\\nFirst, I will recruit the following Supervisors:\\n1.  **Supervisor of UI/UX & Static Content:** To design the overall look, feel, navigation, and structure of the public-facing site.\\n2.  **Supervisor of User Authentication & Secure Zone:** To plan the client-side experience for logging in and accessing the restricted resident area.\\n3.  **Supervisor of Dynamic Features & User Interaction:** To plan the functionality for the contact form, the news feed display, and, crucially, to define the simple, no-code process for updating the news section.\\n\\nI will now issue the recruitment calls to build my team.\\n\\n###recruit-supervisor-ui-ux-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To fulfill the user's requirement for a simple, user-friendly, and easy-to-navigate interface, I need a specialist to plan the overall site structure, visual design, and static page content. This supervisor will create a plan for the sitemap, wireframes, and the HTML/CSS structure for the public-facing informational pages, ensuring a consistent and intuitive user experience.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Supervisor of UI/UX & Static Content\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit-supervisor-ui-ux-01###\\n###recruit-supervisor-auth-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"The project requires a secure, resident-only area. This supervisor will be tasked with planning the entire frontend user authentication flow, including the login page, session management on the client-side, and the structure of the secure portal. This is critical for protecting sensitive HOA information as per the user's request.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Supervisor of User Authentication & Secure Zone\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit-supervisor-auth-01###\\n###recruit-supervisor-dynamic-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To handle the interactive elements of the site like the news feed and contact form, and to design the 'no-code' update process, I need a supervisor focused on client-side logic. This agent will plan the JavaScript necessary to communicate with backend APIs and will create the user-facing documentation for the simple news update system, which is a key user requirement.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"Supervisor of Dynamic Features & User Interaction\\\"\\n}\\n###recruit-supervisor-dynamic-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T00:58:20.217Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 2\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit-supervisor-ui-ux-01\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent J (9) has been recruited with rank Supervisor.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit-supervisor-auth-01\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent C (8) has been recruited with rank Supervisor.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"recruit-supervisor-dynamic-01\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Recruitment approved. New agent Tarkin (7) has been recruited with rank Supervisor.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T00:58:20.217Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. My team is now in place. I have recruited Supervisor J (9) for UI/UX, Supervisor C (8) for Authentication, and Supervisor Tarkin (7) for Dynamic Features.\\n\\nMy next step is to delegate the specific planning tasks to each Supervisor. I will issue precise orders to ensure their individual plans will integrate seamlessly into the comprehensive frontend strategy that I must report to the Chief. Each order will address a specific section of the Chief's original directive.\\n\\nI will now issue the orders to my new subordinates.\\n\\n###order-ui-ux-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor J, your task is to create a plan for the user interface, user experience, and static content of the HOA website. The user's primary requirement is a simple, user-friendly, and easy-to-navigate interface. Your plan must include:\\\\n\\\\n1. A sitemap defining the structure of all public-facing pages (e.g., Home, About, Public News, Contact).\\\\n2. A detailed description of the site's layout, navigation, and visual design principles. Emphasize simplicity and clarity.\\\\n3. A specification of the core frontend technologies (HTML, CSS) and a recommendation for a simple CSS framework to ensure responsiveness.\\\\n\\\\nRecruit attendants as needed to develop wireframes or mockups to support your plan. Report back with this detailed plan.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 9,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ui-ux-plan-01\\\"\\n}\\n###order-ui-ux-01###\\n###order-auth-zone-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor C, you are responsible for planning the client-side implementation of the secure resident-only area. Your plan must detail the user's entire journey related to authentication. This includes:\\\\n\\\\n1. The design of the login page and the user flow for logging in, handling session persistence on the client, and logging out.\\\\n2. The structure and layout of the secure portal, showing how residents will navigate to view protected content like meeting minutes and financial reports.\\\\n3. A plan for how the frontend will interact with the backend authentication services (e.g., API calls to login/logout endpoints).\\\\n\\\\nYour focus is purely on the frontend experience. Recruit as necessary and report back with a complete plan for the secure zone's user experience.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 8,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"auth-zone-plan-01\\\"\\n}\\n###order-auth-zone-01###\\n###order-dynamic-features-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: ORDER\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Tarkin, you will plan the website's dynamic features and user interactions. Your highest priority is the 'no-code' news update system. Your plan must include:\\\\n\\\\n1. A design for the client-side implementation of the contact form, including form fields, validation, and interaction with the backend.\\\\n2. A plan for how the public news feed will be fetched and displayed on the website.\\\\n3. A detailed, step-by-step process for a non-technical user to post a news update. This process must be as simple as possible, for example, by uploading a single text file to a specific, clearly-defined location. Your plan must include user-facing instructions for this process.\\\\n\\\\nRecruit as needed and report back with a comprehensive plan covering these interactive components.\\\",\\n  \\\"recipientAgentID\\\": 7,\\n  \\\"orderSequenceID\\\": \\\"dynamic-features-plan-01\\\"\\n}\\n###order-dynamic-features-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Inspection Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 2\\nType: Approval Request\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Agent C (8) is requesting approval for call ID 8-recruit-1 of type RECRUIT. Payload: {\\\\\\\"justification\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"To create a clear and user-friendly authentication experience, I need a dedicated agent to plan the visual design and user flow of the login page, how user sessions are managed in the browser, and the logout process. This separation of concerns ensures a high-quality user interface.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Authentication UI/UX Attendant\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n}\\n###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"This is a reasonable request from Supervisor C. Delegating the detailed UI/UX planning for the authentication flow to a subordinate is an efficient way to ensure that part of the plan is comprehensive and well-thought-out, directly contributing to the user's requirement for a user-friendly interface. I will approve this recruitment.\\n\\n$$$APPROVED$$$\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Inspection Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 2\\nType: Approval Request\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Agent C (8) is requesting approval for call ID 8-recruit-2 of type RECRUIT. Payload: {\\\\\\\"justification\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The secure portal needs to be intuitively organized so residents can easily find sensitive documents. An agent dedicated to planning the layout, navigation, and presentation of this content is crucial for usability, meeting the user's requirement for a simple interface.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Secure Portal Layout Attendant\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n}\\n###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"This is another logical delegation by Supervisor C. The secure portal's layout is distinct from the public-facing site and requires special attention to ensure residents can easily find the sensitive documents they need. Assigning an attendant to this task is a good management decision. I approve.\\n\\n$$$APPROVED$$$\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Inspection Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 2\\nType: Approval Request\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Agent J (9) is requesting approval for call ID recruit-attendant-sitemap-1 of type RECRUIT. Payload: {\\\\\\\"justification\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"To fulfill order ui-ux-plan-01, I require an Attendant to specialize in the website's information architecture. This agent will be tasked with creating a detailed sitemap and low-fidelity wireframes, ensuring the site's structure is logical, simple, and easy to navigate as per the user's primary requirements.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Attendant for Sitemap and Wireframing\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n}\\n###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"This is a logical and necessary delegation of tasks. Creating a sitemap and wireframes is a foundational step in UI/UX planning and requires focused effort. By recruiting an attendant for this, Supervisor J can ensure the user's primary requirement for an easy-to-navigate interface is met effectively. I approve.\\n\\n$$$APPROVED$$$\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Inspection Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 2\\nType: Approval Request\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Agent C (8) is requesting approval for call ID 8-recruit-3 of type RECRUIT. Payload: {\\\\\\\"justification\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The frontend needs a well-defined plan for communicating with the backend authentication services. This includes detailing the exact API requests for login/logout, handling responses (successes and errors), and managing authentication tokens. A dedicated agent will ensure this logic is robust and secure.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"API Interaction Logic Attendant\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n}\\n###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"This is a crucial technical component of the authentication plan. Having a dedicated agent focus on the specifics of API interaction ensures that the plan is robust, secure, and well-documented. This is an excellent decision by Supervisor C. I approve this recruitment.\\n\\n$$$APPROVED$$$\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Inspection Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 2\\nType: Approval Request\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"Agent J (9) is requesting approval for call ID recruit-attendant-visual-tech-1 of type RECRUIT. Payload: {\\\\\\\"justification\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"To fulfill order ui-ux-plan-01, I require an Attendant to define the website's visual identity and select the appropriate frontend technology. This agent will specify the visual design principles (colors, typography) and recommend a simple, lightweight CSS framework to ensure responsiveness and maintainability, aligning with the user's request for simplicity.\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Attendant for Visual Design and Technology\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n}\\n###APPROVAL_REQUEST###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"This is a sound decision by Supervisor J. Separating the visual design and technology stack from the information architecture allows for specialized focus on both aspects. This will lead to a better, more professional final plan that directly addresses the user's desire for a simple and user-friendly interface. I approve this recruitment.\\n\\n$$$APPROVED$$$\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:01:05.492Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 2\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order-ui-ux-01\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order ui-ux-plan-01 from agent 9: To: Director P\\\\\\\\nFrom: Supervisor J (9)\\\\\\\\nSubject: Completed UI/UX Plan for HOA Website\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis report outlines the comprehensive plan for the user interface, user experience, and static content of the HOA website, as per your order `ui-ux-plan-01`. This plan was developed by synthesizing the detailed reports from my subordinate Attendants, Meero (11) and O (10), who were assigned to information architecture and visual design, respectively.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe resulting plan prioritizes simplicity, clarity, and ease of navigation for a non-technical audience.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 1. Sitemap and Information Architecture\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe following sitemap defines a clear and logical structure for the website, providing intuitive paths to both public information and the secure resident portal.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\nHome (Landing Page)\\\\\\\\n├── About Our HOA\\\\\\\\n├── News & Updates\\\\\\\\n│   └── [Individual News Post Pages]\\\\\\\\n├── Contact Us\\\\\\\\n├── Resident Resources (Public)\\\\\\\\n└── Resident Portal (Login Required)\\\\\\\\n    ├── [Secure Area - Post Login]\\\\\\\\n        ├── Dashboard / Welcome\\\\\\\\n        ├── Meeting Minutes\\\\\\\\n        ├── Financial Reports\\\\\\\\n        └── Account Settings\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 2. Layout, Navigation, and Visual Design\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Layout & Navigation\\\\\\\\n- **Structure:** The site will use a consistent three-part structure on all pages: a header, a main content area, and a footer.\\\\\\\\n- **Header:** Contains the HOA logo and the primary navigation menu (`Home`, `About`, `News`, `Contact`, `Resources`). A prominent `Resident Login` button will be placed here for easy access.\\\\\\\\n- **Content Area:** The layout will be clean and uncluttered, utilizing a card-based design to organize content blocks (e.g., news snippets, contact information). This structure adapts seamlessly to mobile devices, ensuring a responsive experience.\\\\\\\\n- **Footer:** Will contain essential contact details, quick links, and copyright information.\\\\\\\\n- **Navigation Principle:** The navigation is designed to be persistent and predictable. Labels are simple and descriptive, ensuring users can easily find what they are looking for.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Visual Design Principles\\\\\\\\n- **Color Palette:** A professional and calm color scheme has been selected to create a trustworthy feel.\\\\\\\\n    -   **Primary:** `#2C5282` (Deep Blue)\\\\\\\\n    -   **Secondary:** `#3182CE` (Medium Blue)\\\\\\\\n    -   **Accent:** `#48BB78` (Green for buttons and calls-to-action)\\\\\\\\n    -   **Neutrals:** Light grays for backgrounds and dark grays for text to ensure high readability.\\\\\\\\n- **Typography:** A system font stack (e.g., San Francisco, Segoe UI, Roboto, Helvetica Neue) will be used. This approach leverages the native operating system fonts for optimal performance and a familiar feel. A clear typographic hierarchy will be established to differentiate headings from body text.\\\\\\\\n- **Simplicity:** The design will emphasize generous whitespace to reduce cognitive load and improve focus. The use of imagery will be purposeful and minimal, contributing to a clean, professional aesthetic.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 3. Frontend Technology Specification\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Core Technologies:** The website will be built using standard, robust web technologies: **HTML5** and **CSS3**.\\\\\\\\n- **CSS Framework Recommendation:** **Tailwind CSS**.\\\\\\\\n    -   **Justification:** Tailwind CSS is a utility-first framework that is ideal for this project. It allows for rapid development of custom designs without being locked into pre-styled components. Its key advantages include:\\\\\\\\n        -   **Simplicity & Speed:** Utility classes make styling fast and intuitive.\\\\\\\\n        -   **Responsiveness:** Mobile-first design is built-in and easy to implement.\\\\\\\\n        -   **Maintainability:** Styles are co-located with the HTML, making components easy to manage.\\\\\\\\n        -   **Performance:** With tools like PurgeCSS, the final production CSS file is extremely small, leading to faster page loads.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis complete plan provides a clear roadmap for developing a user-friendly, simple, and professional website that meets all stated user requirements.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order-auth-zone-01\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order auth-zone-plan-01 from agent 8: To: Director P (2)\\\\\\\\nFrom: Supervisor C (8)\\\\\\\\nSubject: Complete Plan for Client-Side Secure Zone Implementation\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis report presents the complete, consolidated plan for the client-side implementation of the secure resident-only area, as per your orders. The plan is a synthesis of the detailed work performed by my subordinate Attendants, covering the user experience, portal layout, and technical API integration.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 1. Authentication Experience: Login, Session, and Logout\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe authentication process is designed to be simple for residents while adhering to security best practices.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**A. Login Page & User Flow:**\\\\\\\\n-   **Design:** The login page will be clean and minimal, featuring the HOA logo, a clear \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Resident Portal Login\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" title, fields for username and password, and a single \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Login\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" button. An area for user feedback (e.g., error messages) will be included.\\\\\\\\n-   **Login Process:** Upon clicking \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Login,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" the frontend will perform a `POST` request to the `/api/auth/login` endpoint. \\\\\\\\n    -   On success, the user is redirected to the secure portal dashboard.\\\\\\\\n    -   On failure (e.g., incorrect credentials), a user-friendly error message like \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Invalid username or password\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" is displayed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**B. Session Persistence:**\\\\\\\\n-   To ensure security, session management will employ a robust token-based strategy. A short-lived **access token** will be stored in the browser's memory for authorizing API requests. This mitigates Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) risks. A more durable **refresh token** will be handled by the backend via secure, `HttpOnly` cookies, allowing for seamless session renewal without requiring the user to log in repeatedly.\\\\\\\\n-   Non-sensitive user information (e.g., resident's name) will be stored in `SessionStorage` to personalize the user interface.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**C. Logout Process:**\\\\\\\\n-   A clearly visible \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Logout\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" button will be available in the main portal navigation.\\\\\\\\n-   Clicking this button will clear all client-side session data (in-memory tokens, `SessionStorage`) and trigger a `POST` request to a `/api/auth/logout` endpoint. This allows the backend to invalidate the session, providing a definitive and secure logout.\\\\\\\\n-   The user will then be redirected to the public homepage or the login page.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 2. Secure Portal Structure and Layout\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe secure portal is structured to provide intuitive navigation to protected content.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n-   **Sitemap:** The portal will be organized logically, with a main dashboard (`/portal/dashboard`) and clear sections for documents (`/portal/documents/meeting-minutes`, `/portal/documents/financial-reports`), news, and account management.\\\\\\\\n-   **Dashboard:** After logging in, the resident lands on a dashboard that serves as a central hub. It will feature a welcome message and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"quick access cards\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" linking directly to the most important content, such as the latest meeting minutes and financial reports.\\\\\\\\n-   **Content Pages:** Pages displaying lists of documents will use a consistent, easy-to-read table format with columns for title, date, and a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"View/Download\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" link.\\\\\\\\n-   **Navigation:** A persistent primary navigation bar ([Dashboard], [Documents], [News]) and breadcrumbs (e.g., `Portal > Documents > Meeting Minutes`) will ensure residents can always orient themselves within the portal.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### 3. Frontend & Backend API Interaction Plan\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe frontend will communicate with the backend via a well-defined API protocol.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n-   **Authentication Endpoints:** The primary interactions will be with `POST /api/auth/login` and `POST /api/auth/logout`.\\\\\\\\n-   **Accessing Protected Resources:** To view secure content (e.g., meeting minutes), the frontend will make authenticated requests to the appropriate API endpoints (e.g., `GET /api/documents/meeting-minutes`).\\\\\\\\n-   **Authorization:** Every request to a protected endpoint will include the in-memory access token in the `Authorization: Bearer {accessToken}` header. The frontend logic will handle the process of automatically refreshing this token using the secure refresh token when it expires, ensuring a smooth user experience.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis comprehensive plan provides a complete blueprint for developing a secure, user-friendly, and robust client-side experience for the HOA's resident portal. My team is prepared for the next phase.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"order-dynamic-features-01\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Report for order dynamic-features-plan-01 from agent 7: **Comprehensive Plan – Website Dynamic Features & “No-Code” News Update System**\\\\\\\\nSupervisor: Tarkin (rank 3)\\\\\\\\nOrder Sequence ID: dynamic-features-plan-01\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Contact Form (Client-side)\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nA. Form Fields\\\\\\\\n   • Full Name  (required, min 2 chars)\\\\\\\\n   • Email      (required, RFC 5322 pattern)\\\\\\\\n   • Subject    (required, 3–80 chars)\\\\\\\\n   • Message    (required, 10–2 000 chars)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. UI/UX & Validation\\\\\\\\n   • Use semantic <form> / <label> / <input> / <textarea>.  \\\\\\\\n   • Native HTML5 validation attributes (required, minlength, maxlength, pattern) as first line of defence.  \\\\\\\\n   • Lightweight JS (≈40 LOC) for live feedback & graceful fallback.\\\\\\\\n   • Disable submit button until all fields pass validity checks.\\\\\\\\n   • Display inline error hints and an overall status banner.\\\\\\\\n   • Accessibility: ARIA-live region for status, tab order, colour-agnostic error icons.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Backend Interaction\\\\\\\\n   • JS fetch() POSTs JSON → API Gateway endpoint /contact.  \\\\\\\\n   • On success (200), show ‘Message sent!’ toast and reset form; on error, show ‘Retry’ banner.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nD. Security\\\\\\\\n   • CORS allow only site origin.  \\\\\\\\n   • reCAPTCHA-v3 or AWS WAF CAPTCHA (optional, switchable).  \\\\\\\\n   • API key throttling (10 req/min/IP) at API Gateway.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n2. Public News Feed (Client consumption)\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nA. Data Shape (feed.json in S3)\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n[\\\\\\\\n  {\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":   \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-05-14-budget-approved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024 Budget Approved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"date\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2024-05-14\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"author\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"HOA Board\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Homeowners voted…\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"contentUrl\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/news/2024-05-14-budget-approved.html\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  },\\\\\\\\n  …\\\\\\\\n]\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. Fetch & Render Logic\\\\\\\\n   • At page load, JS fetch('/news/feed.json') (served via CloudFront, max-age 300s).  \\\\\\\\n   • Map over array → build list items (date-sorted, newest first).  \\\\\\\\n   • Clicking an item navigates to pre-generated HTML (see §3.C.3).  \\\\\\\\n   • If fetch fails, show cached copy (localStorage) or friendly error.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Styling / Progressive Enhancement\\\\\\\\n   • Feed container initially shows CSS loader → replaced once data arrives.  \\\\\\\\n   • For users w/o JS, server provides static /news/index.html (optionally CloudFront Function to inject).  \\\\\\\\n   • All links, colours, typography inherit from design system tokens.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n3. “No-Code” News Update System\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\nGoal: Non-technical user adds news by uploading ONE plain-text/Markdown file; everything else is automatic.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA. Infrastructure (server-side)\\\\\\\\n   1. S3 bucket: hoa-news-uploads   (private)\\\\\\\\n      • Prefix: incoming/\\\\\\\\n   2. S3 bucket: hoa-website-public (existing site)\\\\\\\\n      • Prefix: news/\\\\\\\\n   3. Lambda function: NewsPublisher\\\\\\\\n      Trigger: s3:ObjectCreated:* on hoa-news-uploads/incoming/\\\\\\\\n   4. IAM role: lambda-news-publisher with write access to public bucket.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nB. File Naming Convention (simple)\\\\\\\\n   • YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md   e.g. 2024-05-14-budget-approved.md\\\\\\\\n   • First Markdown H1 (# Title) becomes title; first blank-line-separated paragraph becomes summary.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nC. Lambda Processing Steps\\\\\\\\n   1. Parse filename → id, date.  \\\\\\\\n   2. Read Markdown → convert to HTML with showdown.js.  \\\\\\\\n   3. Output files to public bucket:\\\\\\\\n      a. news/<id>.html  (full article)\\\\\\\\n      b. (Re)generate news/feed.json (pull last N=100 posts, already in bucket + this one, date sort).\\\\\\\\n   4. Delete or archive original upload to uploads/processed/.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nD. Time to Live / Caching\\\\\\\\n   • feed.json & article pages: Cache-Control: max-age=300, s-maxage=300 (CloudFront).  \\\\\\\\n   • Lambda invalidates CloudFront paths /news/feed.json & /news/<id>.html on publish.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nE. Error Handling & Notifications\\\\\\\\n   • On Lambda error, move file to uploads/failed/ and send SES email to site admin with stacktrace.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nF. Cost Estimate\\\\\\\\n   • S3 PUT & GET (few cents/month).  \\\\\\\\n   • Lambda runtime ~1 000 ms per post (<$0.01/month).  \\\\\\\\n   • No database required.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nG. Security\\\\\\\\n   • Upload bucket is private; user authenticates via AWS Console or Pre-Signed POST (see Option B below).  \\\\\\\\n   • Lambda runs in tight VPC-less configuration; no internet egress needed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nH. User-Facing Instructions (Option A – AWS Console)\\\\\\\\n–––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––\\\\\\\\nStep 1 – Write Your News\\\\\\\\n• Open any text editor.  \\\\\\\\n• Start with a line: `# Your Headline Here`  \\\\\\\\n• Add an empty line.  \\\\\\\\n• Write 1-2 sentences summary.  \\\\\\\\n• Add another empty line, then full article in Markdown.  \\\\\\\\n• Save file using the suggested name format (e.g. 2024-05-14-summer-picnic.md).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 2 – Upload the File\\\\\\\\n• Sign in to AWS Console → S3.  \\\\\\\\n• Open bucket “hoa-news-uploads”.  \\\\\\\\n• Click into folder “incoming”.  \\\\\\\\n• Press “Upload” → “Add files” → choose your .md file → “Upload”.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStep 3 – Done!\\\\\\\\n• Wait ~5 seconds.  \\\\\\\\n• Refresh your public website; the new story appears in the News list.  \\\\\\\\n• If it doesn’t appear after 1 min, check email for error notice.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI. User-Facing Instructions (Option B – Internal Upload Page – *future enhancement*)\\\\\\\\n• We can create a simple password-protected page that returns a pre-signed S3 POST; user drags file, presses ‘Publish’, done.  (Not in MVP but planned.)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n4. Summary of Deliverables for Execution Phase\\\\\\\\n────────────────────────────────────────\\\\\\\\n1. Front-end code\\\\\\\\n   • /contact.html & contact.js (≈150 LOC)  \\\\\\\\n   • /news/news.js (≈120 LOC)\\\\\\\\n2. S3 buckets, Lambda, API Gateway, IAM roles, CloudFront invalidations.\\\\\\\\n3. Lambda-NewsPublisher (Node.js 18, 120 LOC incl. markdown parser).\\\\\\\\n4. Documentation snippets pasted above for HOA admins.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPlan is deterministic: all resource names, prefixes and file names defined; minimal moving parts; no databases; low cost; strong security; end-user process is 3 clicks.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n–Supervisor Tarkin\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-22T01:01:05.492Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. My Supervisors have returned with comprehensive, high-quality plans that cover all aspects of my directive. Supervisor J's UI/UX plan provides a solid foundation, Supervisor C's authentication plan ensures security and usability for the resident portal, and Supervisor Tarkin's dynamic features plan, especially the no-code news update system, is an elegant solution to a key user requirement.\\n\\nThe individual plans are cohesive and integrate perfectly. I will now synthesize these reports into a single, comprehensive frontend plan and report back to the Chief to fulfill my original order.\\n\\n###final-frontend-plan-report-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"To: Chief Delta (1)\\\\nFrom: Director P (2)\\\\nSubject: Comprehensive Frontend & User Experience Plan for HOA Website\\\\n\\\\nThis report presents the complete and final plan for the HOA website's frontend and user experience, per your order `frontend-plan-01`. This plan is a synthesis of the detailed reports from my subordinate Supervisors—J (UI/UX), C (Authentication), and Tarkin (Dynamic Features)—and is designed to meet all user requirements for a simple, secure, and easy-to-manage website.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 1: Site Architecture, UI, and Visual Design**\\\\n\\\\nThis section, derived from Supervisor J's work, defines the overall look, feel, and structure.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Sitemap:** A logical site structure has been defined, including public pages (Home, About, News, Contact) and a clear entry point to the secure Resident Portal.\\\\n*   **Layout & Navigation:** The design specifies a consistent, responsive layout with a persistent header, main content area, and footer. Navigation will be simple and intuitive. The primary technology stack will be **HTML5**, **CSS3**, and the **Tailwind CSS** framework for its speed, maintainability, and ability to create a clean, custom design.\\\\n*   **Visual Design:** A professional color palette (blues, greens, grays) and a standard system font stack will be used to ensure readability, a trustworthy aesthetic, and optimal performance.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 2: Secure Resident Portal & Authentication**\\\\n\\\\nThis section, based on Supervisor C's plan, details the client-side experience for the secure resident-only area.\\\\n\\\\n*   **User Authentication Flow:** The login process will be straightforward, with a minimal login page. On the backend, we will use a secure token-based strategy (in-memory access tokens, HttpOnly refresh tokens) to manage sessions, ensuring both security and a smooth user experience.\\\\n*   **Secure Portal Structure:** After login, residents will land on a dashboard with quick access to key documents. The portal will feature clear navigation to sections like Meeting Minutes and Financial Reports, presented in an easy-to-scan table format.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:** The frontend will communicate with a well-defined set of API endpoints for login, logout, and fetching protected data, with all secure requests being properly authorized.\\\\n\\\\n**Part 3: Dynamic Features & No-Code Content Management**\\\\n\\\\nThis section, based on Supervisor Tarkin's detailed plan, covers the site's interactive elements and fulfills the critical 'no-code' update requirement.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Contact Form:** A standard contact form with client-side validation will provide a simple way for users to send messages, which will be submitted to a secure backend endpoint.\\\\n*   **News Feed:** The public news feed will be dynamically populated by fetching a simple `feed.json` file from S3, ensuring fast load times and easy updates.\\\\n*   **'No-Code' News Update System:** This is the cornerstone of the user-friendly management experience. A non-technical user can post a new article by following a simple, two-step process:\\\\n    1.  **Write the article:** Create a simple text file in Markdown format, with the filename `YYYY-MM-DD-your-title.md`.\\\\n    2.  **Upload the file:** Using the AWS Console, upload this single file to a specific S3 bucket (`hoa-news-uploads/incoming/`).\\\\n*   **Automation:** An AWS Lambda function will automatically trigger on upload, convert the Markdown to HTML, generate the new article page, update the `feed.json` file, and clear the CloudFront cache. The new article will appear on the public website within seconds.\\\\n\\\\n**Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nThis comprehensive plan provides a complete, deterministic roadmap for developing the website's frontend. It directly addresses all user requirements for simplicity, security, and ease of use, with special attention paid to the non-technical content management process. My team and I are ready to proceed to the execution phase upon your approval.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"frontend-plan-01\\\"\\n}\\n###final-frontend-plan-report-01###\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"custom\":{}}],\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"Excellent. My Supervisors have returned with comprehensive, high-quality plans that cover all aspects of my directive. Supervisor J's UI/UX plan provides a solid foundation, Supervisor C's authentication plan ensures security and usability for the resident portal, and Supervisor Tarkin's dynamic features plan, especially the no-code news update system, is an elegant solution to a key user requirement.\\n\\nThe individual plans are cohesive and integrate perfectly. I will now synthesize these reports into a single, comprehensive frontend plan and report back to the Chief to fulfill my original order.\\n\\n###final-frontend-plan-report-01###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"To: Chief Delta (1)\\\\nFrom: Director P (2)\\\\nSubject: Comprehensive Frontend & User Experience Plan for HOA Website\\\\n\\\\nThis report